WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.538 --> 00:00:02.057
- [Voiceover] You know what this is.

00:00:02.057 --> 00:00:05.050
The wagon, the river, dying of dysentary.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:06.229
All you ever wanted to do was

00:00:06.229 --> 00:00:08.574
get to Willamette Valley safely.

00:00:08.574 --> 00:00:10.609
It's the Oregon Trail, but did you know

00:00:10.609 --> 00:00:14.892
that this iconic game
started as a board game?

00:00:14.892 --> 00:00:18.382
It was 1971, three guys,
Don, Bill, and Paul

00:00:18.382 --> 00:00:20.329
were roommates in Minnesota.

00:00:20.329 --> 00:00:21.797
They were student teachers.

00:00:21.797 --> 00:00:24.399
Don, who would often dress
up as historical figures

00:00:24.399 --> 00:00:26.699
to captivate his students,
needed a creative way

00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:29.525
to teach lessons about the Oregon Trail.

00:00:29.525 --> 00:00:31.098
So he created a board game.

00:00:31.098 --> 00:00:33.654
He started by using a map
of the American frontier

00:00:33.654 --> 00:00:36.502
and wrote flash cards for
the hardships pioneers faced,

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:40.750
like dysentery, broken wagon
wheels, and snake bites.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.068
As soon as his roommates
got wind of the board game,

00:00:43.068 --> 00:00:44.479
they suggested he pump it up by

00:00:44.479 --> 00:00:46.105
turning it into a computer game.

00:00:46.105 --> 00:00:48.172
Over the next two weeks,
the three men put together

00:00:48.172 --> 00:00:50.938
the very first version of Oregon Trail.

00:00:50.938 --> 00:00:53.418
But, small problem: they
didn't have a computer,

00:00:53.418 --> 00:00:56.031
so they used this wacky
machine, a teletype,

00:00:56.031 --> 00:00:58.053
a sort of glorified typewriter

00:00:58.053 --> 00:00:59.789
that didn't even have a monitor.

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:01.090
Basically, they would type code

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.880
onto these huge pieces of
paper and then print them out.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.711
When the program was done, Don's class

00:01:06.711 --> 00:01:08.370
couldn't get enough of the game.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.465
Kids were staying late and arriving early

00:01:10.465 --> 00:01:12.537
just so they could play Oregon Trail.

00:01:12.537 --> 00:01:14.575
Several years later, Don got a job

00:01:14.575 --> 00:01:17.530
with the Minnesota Educational
Computing Consortion,

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.459
an educational software company

00:01:19.459 --> 00:01:22.087
and he pitched them Oregon
Trail as a program idea.

00:01:22.087 --> 00:01:24.607
As soon as Apple 2s were
launched in the late ‘70s

00:01:24.607 --> 00:01:27.282
and sent out to classrooms
all over Minnesota,

00:01:27.282 --> 00:01:29.443
Oregon Trail became a big hit.

00:01:29.443 --> 00:01:32.815
Such a big hit that other
states began licensing the game.

00:01:32.815 --> 00:01:35.982
And the rest, as they say, is history.

